{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfMi5ccE1XsDWQcMwm8SJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[{\"unitKey\":\"20240818-theory-q17a\",\"questionNumber\":17,\"part\":\"a\",\"status\":\"PASS\",\"textIssues\":[],\"choiceIssues\":[],\"answerIssues\":[],\"explanationIssues\":[],\"figureIssues\":[],\"sourceIssues\":[],\"verifiedEvidence\":[\"I compared the (a) question text with the official original page (printed page -20-, T1-R 023). The wording, the Δ and Y terms, and the unit [μF] all match.\",\"All five choices match the original: (1) 1.0, (2) 2.0, (3) 4.5, (4) 6.0, (5) 9.0.\",\"The official answer table (R6 first half, theory, 問17(a)) gives 5, which matches officialAnswer=5.\",\"Figure 2 in the original: terminal b goes to the top vertex, c to the bottom-left vertex and d to the bottom-right vertex. The left side (b-c), right side (b-d) and bottom side (c-d) are each 3μF. The public candidate figure shows the same connections, labels and values.\",\"Figure 3 in the original: capacitors of C[μF] run from an unlabelled centre node to b, c and d. The candidate figure matches.\",\"Recalculation: with c open, the Δ side between b and d is 3 + 3·3/(3+3) = 4.5μF. The Y side is C/2. Setting them equal gives C = 9.0μF. This agrees with the symmetric-network rule C_Y = 3C_Δ.\",\"Symmetry makes all three terminal pairs give the same condition, so checking one pair (b-d) is enough. The explanation's reasoning is sound.\",\"Each wrong choice has a correct reason. (1) applies the resistor rule R_Y = R_Δ/3 to capacitors, giving 3/3 = 1.0. (2) gives C/2 = 1.0, which does not equal 4.5. (3) confuses the Δ-side combined capacitance 4.5 with C. (4) gives C/2 = 3.0, which does not equal 4.5.\",\"officialReferenceUrls is empty, so there were no independent claims to check against primary sources. The page numbers [22, 23] match the T1-R 022/023 codes on the original pages.\"]},{\"unitKey\":\"20240818-theory-q17b\",\"questionNumber\":17,\"part\":\"b\",\"status\":\"PASS\",\"textIssues\":[],\"choiceIssues\":[],\"answerIssues\":[],\"explanationIssues\":[],\"figureIssues\":[],\"sourceIssues\":[],\"verifiedEvidence\":[\"I compared the (b) question text with the original page. The references to Figure 1 and Figure 3, the symbol C0 and the unit [μF] all match.\",\"All five choices match the original: (1) 3.0, (2) 4.5, (3) 4.8, (4) 6.0, (5) 9.0.\",\"The official answer table gives 3 for 問17(b), which matches officialAnswer=3.\",\"Figure 1 in the original: the junction on the a side connects to b through 9μF and to c through 18μF. b and c each connect to the junction on the d side through 3μF, and b-c is 3μF. The public candidate figure shows the same connections, values and labels.\",\"b-d, c-d and b-c are all 3μF, so the Δ part between b, c and d is the same as Figure 2. Replacing it with the Y network from (a) gives b-o = c-o = d-o = 9μF.\",\"Recalculation: the a-b-o path is 9·9/18 = 4.5μF and the a-c-o path is 18·9/27 = 6.0μF. In parallel they give 10.5μF. In series with o-d (9μF): C0 = 94.5/19.5 = 4.846…μF, closest to 4.8.\",\"Each wrong choice has a correct reason. (1) drops the a-c-o path: 4.5·9/13.5 = 3.0. (2) and (4) are partial results for a single path. (5) confuses the answer with C from (a).\",\"officialReferenceUrls is empty, so there were no independent claims to check against primary sources.\"]},{\"unitKey\":\"20240818-theory-q18a\",\"questionNumber\":18,\"part\":\"a\",\"status\":\"PASS\",\"textIssues\":[],\"choiceIssues\":[],\"answerIssues\":[],\"explanationIssues\":[],\"figureIssues\":[],\"sourceIssues\":[],\"verifiedEvidence\":[\"I compared the (a) passage with the original page (printed page -21-, T1-R 024). The positions of blanks (ア) to (エ) and the terms AM, FM and PM all match.\",\"All five combinations match the original table: (1) 高い/信号波/三角波/検波, (2) 高い/信号波/正弦波/検波, (3) 高い/搬送波/三角波/増幅, (4) 低い/信号波/三角波/増幅, (5) 低い/搬送波/正弦波/検波.\",\"The official answer table gives 2 for 問18(a), which matches officialAnswer=2.\",\"Content check: the carrier has a higher frequency than the signal wave, the receiver extracts the signal-wave component, AM/FM/PM use a sine-wave carrier, and demodulation is also called detection (検波). This agrees with the combination in (2).\",\"For every wrong choice, the explanation correctly identifies which blanks are wrong.\",\"(a) can be answered from the text alone. The note that Figures 1 and 2 are used only in (b) matches the original layout, and figureUrls being empty is appropriate.\",\"officialReferenceUrls is empty, so there were no independent claims to check against primary sources. The page numbers [24, 25] match the T1-R 024/025 codes on the original pages.\"]}]"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3566,"cache_read_input_tokens":169928,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3566},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cd8b495d-ee1c-4b95-a5cd-306a63cd83cd","uuid":"67670bbe-3e9a-4d3f-81c3-f55813438c2e","timestamp":"2026-09-24T05:26:17.075Z","request_id":"req_011CfMi5c2kCpzPb9BjXvZ9t"}
